From 0455c2fbe3f6a1161d56e2f1db474152c5c6a1b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aegis Development <development@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 20:40:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(android): bind Access Options editor to a menu grant in
 its window

Issue the editor only to the receiver created by a browser menu action, once
that receiver is attached to the tab window. The menu grant is one-use and is
bound to the receiver, tab contents, primary main frame and window, so revoked
or restored receivers cannot obtain a new editor through a direct native call.
Run the instrumentation by default in an internal-only APK that carries the
product identity and product template resources.

The regression skips the first run experience and recreates the real browser
Activity, so the restored receiver is the one Android rebuilds from saved
state rather than a dialog shown by the test.

The test library depends only on test support targets rather than
:chrome_test_java_helper, so the dedicated APK carries no other runnable suite.

The menu grant follows the existing trust boundary: configuration files,
Intents, deep links and Java-supplied channel values never grant privileges,
while browser-process code is trusted. It keeps revoked, restored or reused
receivers from obtaining an editor; it does not defend against same-process
callers.

A receiver authorized for the original tab and then attached to another window
cannot open that tab's editor. The regression keeps the original editor
readable, so the rejection is the window check rather than an expired handle
or a missing menu grant.

A receiver attached to another window cannot read the original editor handle, while the owning receiver still can.

When Fragment restoration is suppressed, native resume shows the unavailable dialog from the hint saved by the Activity and does not issue an editor. The regression recreates the Activity through that production path, so removing the hint capture or the resume hook fails the test.

The regression opens the activity menu and clicks the displayed Access Options row, so a menu that no longer dispatches that item fails. Normal recreation must restore the existing dialog. Only suppressed Fragment restoration may create the unavailable dialog from the saved hint.

Destroying the dialog view closes the editor. Fragment detach does not run onDestroy, so a reattached receiver cannot reuse the old handle. The regression detaches and reattaches that same receiver.

A detached or dialog-less receiver is not a visible dialog. Restoration between
detach and reattach removes that tag and shows unavailable from the saved hint.
The menu can then open a new editor. The regression recreates the Activity
while the receiver is detached, dismisses the restored dialog, and opens the
menu again.
